  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Cornelius Vanderbilt Crane inherited wealth created by the Crane family’s plumbing and industrial manufacturing business. Rather than enter its management, he became a naturalist, explorer and scientific benefactor. He financed and led a Field Museum expedition around the Pacific, assembling a specialist team whose work produced extensive zoological collections and later museum publications.\n\nCrane continued combining travel with philanthropy. In French Polynesia he acquired and restored the neglected Harrison Smith botanical garden, arranging for part of it to pass into public ownership. He also supported research against filariasis and elephantiasis and organised a further natural-history expedition to the Marquesas. These projects turned inherited industrial wealth into a practical legacy of specimen collection, tropical medicine and conservation.",
    "html": "<p>Cornelius Vanderbilt Crane inherited wealth created by the Crane family’s plumbing and industrial manufacturing business. Rather than enter its management, he became a naturalist, explorer and scientific benefactor. He financed and led a Field Museum expedition around the Pacific, assembling a specialist team whose work produced extensive zoological collections and later museum publications.</p>\r\n<p>Crane continued combining travel with philanthropy. In French Polynesia he acquired and restored the neglected Harrison Smith botanical garden, arranging for part of it to pass into public ownership. He also supported research against filariasis and elephantiasis and organised a further natural-history expedition to the Marquesas. These projects turned inherited industrial wealth into a practical legacy of specimen collection, tropical medicine and conservation.</p>\r\n",
